nformation now  yeah i believe that any information that  does not jeopardize  either the attorney general's case or  the fbi case  should now be open for the public to see  to be informed  about all the facts in the case again  that don't jeopardize or imperil any  criminal investigations  so we call on everybody that has that  information to to release  it um  amina allahi from wfpl asks  protesters have demanded that sergeant  jonathan mattingly and detective miles  cosgrove  also be fired mayor fischer will you  fire them  well if at the end of the investigation  if there's a fireball offense  certainly that would be the right thing  to do  now there is a procedure that's outlined  in state law  with the police officers bill of rights  and the fop con  contract it defines what that whole  process looks like so if at the end of  that process that's the case  that's the action to take that action  would be taken  the next question is from nick vogel  burrows from the new york times  and i think this is for chief schroeder  have officers arrested mr hankinson did  he turn himself in  roughly how many people have been  arrested so far in the protest  and what are most people being arrested  for  so as far as detective hankinson he's  not been arrested by members of the  louisville metro police department  as the investigating agency  the attorney general's office would be  the one to coordinate that i do not have  that information  as far as um beyond the protest the  situation is still ongoing  several protesters have been arrested in  the area of bardstown and midland  that situation is currently ongoing as  well i don't have those numbers  nor do i currently have the charges that  they've been arrested for but we can get  that information later  okay the next question is for the mayor  from  christy shank of wave three she asked if  the officers were  found were not found responsible for  brianna's death why would the city  settle  for 12 million dollars it's important  for us to begin the healing process  uh for the city uh for all the community  that we have here in the country as well  so  i believe it was the right decision to  make at the right time and of course  remember there was also police reforms  that were part of that settlement so  that we could have more transparency  more accountability with our police  department it's important that we have  community legitimacy uh with trust with  the police department so that was also  part  of that whole  we have process question from amina  allahi of wfpl  do you agree mayor fisher do you agree  with the attorney general's findings  well i i mean i support the grand jury  process  obviously i've not been privy to look at  all of the information that was part of  the process but i respect the process  and  where it's taking us right now so as the  information that was considered  comes out in the future we'll have the  opportunity to weigh on on whether or  not  i agree with what the resolution of the  case was  or chief schroeder chief  what has been the response inside the  department  to sergeant mattingly's letter sent to  officers  so sergeant mattingly's email we are  aware of the email and we're currently  looking at the situation  it was an email sent that represents his  views not the views of the department  uh each officer would have their own  individual response and  and i can't speak for the majority of  officers on how they feel  about that email  one more question from amina from wfpl  some are concerned militia type groups  may not be treated the same as other  protesters by police would you describe  how lmpd will approach policing the  various groups  we approach policing the various groups  the same we don't judge groups by their  political beliefs  by what side of the political spectrum  they're on  how we judge it is exclusively on the  behavior they demonstrate we  we judge and act upon the behavior we  observe  not from whatever political stance a  group may take  thank you there are no other questions  this afternoon obviously a big day for  our city  and the nation i just want to remind  everybody our country has been here  before  some 50-plus years ago uh there's been  protests before there have been cries  for racial justice  before because racism has haunted our  nation since its founding days  unless all of us commit to ending racism  we will be here again but our history is  not our destiny  while we have much work to do this is  also a time i believe of great hope  for our city and our country i'm  convinced that this movement that's  shaking our city and our country will  lead to a better tomorrow  and that's the work ahead of us let's  seize this opportunity i understand it's  not easy for everybody to do this  but when we're able to look back on this  day and on this time in our history  we need to be able to say we responded  to this call for more equity  to finally work on ending structural  systemic racism  in our country it's our opportunity to  seize that now  we have our full commitment here in the  city of louisville to do that thank you  you
 

